@Special option menu
You can optionally define a special option menu. This will be available
during the game by pushing the F8 key.

You can define the action to perform when pushing F8, which is normally a
menu, although it can also be a help file or a single option. (If it is a
menu, then the menu can contain these actions as well as submenus.)

The possible actions which can be performed are:
!me;Menu
!od;Option
!he;Help
!so;Sound

Inside of a menu, the following additional actions are available:
!can;Cancel menu
!go;Go to page
!pb;Page break

:me;Menu definition
A menu has one or more pages, with a screen number for each one. Each page
has any number of menu items.

In the menu editor, there is the display: <X> (00,00)[00] where <X> means
the key code, (00,00) are the X and Y coordinates within the screen where
it should be displayed, and [00] is the field width. If it is displayed,
then the value of that menu item is displayed there, and the screen must
use text/numbers at that position to be able to display it, as explained
below. Specify 0 as the field width to not display it. After that, will
be displayed › if it is auto-cancel or ú if not. Auto-cancel means that
selecting that menu item will automatically cancel this menu in addition
to the other effects; it is redundant if the type of menu item is cancel.

Key codes for menu items must be unique within each page, and must be a
letter A to Z or a digit 0 to 9. Those are the keys on the keyboard which
will be used to activate those menu items.

The screen of a menu can only use the following tiles: background, text,
and numeric (context-specific). Text and numeric are only used in the
positions within the field according to the menu item. For numeric tiles,
only decimal and comma formats can be used.

Window definitions can also be used. The "fixed item" colour is used for
locked fields, and the "key item" colour is used for variable fields, and
the "normal item" colour is used for any other fields (if these colours
are not set, then the secondary colour of the screen grid is used).

:od;Option definition
The info number is from 0 to 32767. This number must be different from all
other info numbers within the world file, and it is recommended to use
numbers consecutively starting from zero (although they are not required
to be in numerical order).

There are two kind of options, being numeric and enumerated. If it is
numeric, then you can specify the valid range. If it is enumerated, then
you can specify each possible value, with its name and its numeric value.
In either case, the value must be in range 0 to 65535, and you must also
specify the default value.

You must also specify a screen number for the option. This screen number is
the screen for setting the value of the option.
!wn;Screen for numeric option
!wo;Screen for enumerated option

You can optionally define OIDs for an option. These can be used to share
options between game worlds (if the user defines the values by OIDs in the
user configuration file), and/or to have meanings that are common between
multiple game worlds (such as many kind of accessibility settings). If
there is more than one, then the first usable one counts. Enter ... at the
beginning for a relative OID (most will probably be relative).
!oid;OIDs

The flags are:

þ Lockable þ If selected, then this option is locked after the first time
it is read by the program in the game world; at this point, the user cannot
make any more changes to this option. If it is not selected, then it can be
changed by the user at any time.

þ Variable þ Allows the value to potentially vary automatically during the
game, if any of the OIDs are applicable for this use. If not selected, then
the OIDs can only be used to program the initial value.

þ Save þ Causes the value to be stored in save games. It always stores the
value in save games if it is locked (see above), and the user configuration
can override this setting.

:wn;Screen for numeric option
The screen for a numeric option uses backgrounds and numbers; others are
not used. The title can be displayed in the message area, if it is present.
The only numbers used in a numeric option screen are:

þ Line number þ The minimum value
þ Line count þ The maximum value
þ Context-specific þ The current value

The only numeric formats that are available are decimal and commas.

The number keys, as well as up/down arrows, and plus/minus, backspace,
delete, can be used to adjust the value, followed by return or escape.

The fixed item colour is used if this option is locked, and the vacant
item colour is used if an invalid number has been entered (which is only
possible if the number keys and backspace are used; you must enter a valid
number before it can be accepted).

:wo;Screen for enumerated option
This screen works like a text window, but cannot be scrollable, and some
of the other features of text windows are also unavailable. Like with
numeric options, the title can be displayed in the message area if present.

The valid screen elements are: backgrounds, text windows, cursor indicator,
and numbers as follows: line number, line count, and context-specific (the
value of the selected option).

The only numeric formats that are available are decimal and commas.

The selected item colour is also used for the currently selected value, and
the fixed item colour is used if this option is currently locked.

You can use up/down arrows, return, escape, and letters/numbers to select
the choices in the menu.

:oid;OIDs
If you want to define your own OIDs, you must first register a OID for
yourself; you can ask the maintainer of Super ZZ Zero for a OID and then
you can define your own OIDs that use that one as a prefix. (For example,
if 2.999.1 is assigned to you, then you may use 2.999.1.14.0 as a OID.
However, the OIDs which will be assigned will start with "...1."; this is
just an example that it doesn't do that.) (It is also possible to get OIDs
in other ways, such by converting UUIDs to the 2.25 OID arc, or getting a
OID from other registration authorities, but using the Super ZZ Zero OID
arc as described above will result in a more compact encoding because it
is possible to use relative OIDs.) You should avoid using the same full
OID for multiple purposes.

(There are no OIDs currently defined for this purpose.)

:he;Help
Specify the screen number to use for the text window that displays the help
file, and also specify the name of the help lump (excluding .HLP).

:so;Sound
This can be used for making a sound test menu.

Specify the sound in the same format as the #PLAY command. It will always
override an existing sound regardless of priority.

:can;Cancel menu
No parameters need to be specified. This will cancel the menu. (The escape
key will also cancel the menu, so this is not strictly required.)

:go;Go to page
Specify the zero-based page number within the menu. (The page up and page
down keys can also be used to access different pages within a menu, so this
is not strictly required.)

:pb;Page break
Specify the screen number for the page after this break. (There must be a
page break at the beginning of each menu, which cannot be deleted, nor can
its type be changed.)

See the section about menu defintions for more information.
!me;Menu definition
